The merged ticket covers different mechanisms. The parser fix does not fix the booking form's
misleading hint. Keep one engineering ticket per cause, with report IDs and a count for each.
These are findings from the supplied desk model and export; the product implementation and its
fixes are not included here.

<Callout kind="warn">
The old “one issue, one fix” claim is contradicted by the running view below.
Use tenant locale, booking form and channel to place reports. Do not merge on subject or intake tag alone.
</Callout>

## Cause-based tickets

<CauseTable ticket={4471} issues={issueLinks} />

### Parser: month-first parsing of day-first input

The shared parser reads month first where the tenant writes day first. App and email reports,
and forms with a compatible day-first hint, follow this path in `causeOf`.
For example, `R-41200` reports 7 April becoming 4 July at `harbour.dental` (`en-GB`).
Correct the parsing contract to the tenant's format, validate impossible dates, and verify the
intended calendar date survives storage and invitation generation.

### Hint: booking-v3 teaches the wrong order

For a day-first tenant, booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while its form reads dates in the tenant's order.
For example, `R-41230` at `copper.studio` (`es-ES`) reports 6 January becoming 1 June.
Typing `01/06` as instructed for 6 January can be read as 1 June. Align the hint and the reader,
then test form submissions for each configured locale. Correcting the shared parser alone leaves this path wrong.

<ReviewFindings />

## Received and merged are different events

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Before the next merge

1. Keep the report ID, body, received time, tenant and channel.
2. Look up the tenant's locale and form, and inspect the mechanism. An unknown tenant stays unplaced.
3. Match to an existing ticket only when the cause matches. A shared subject does not establish that.
4. Keep a separate count of distinct IDs for each cause and investigate contradictory symptoms.
5. Recheck incoming reports after each fix. A change in merge throughput cannot demonstrate recovery.

Try the actual cause comparison below. The same subject can describe different failures; different
subjects can describe the same failure.

